== Notes ==
* [[Piercing]] tears make the fight much easier, as tears can pass through Carrion Queen's invulnerable segments and hit her vulnerable segment.

== Trivia ==
* Carrion Queen was added in the ''Wrath of the Lamb'' DLC for the original ''The Binding of Isaac''.
* In the flash version, the Carrion Queen could also be encountered in {{s|Necropolis}}.
* She is the posthumous version of {{e|Chub}}.
** Since the Carrion Queen is a posthumous version of Chub, and both appear in Chapter 2, this makes Chub one of four bosses to have its poshumous version in the same chapter the others being {{e|Gemini}} having {{e|Blighted Ovum}} in [[Chapter 1]], {{e|Polycephalus}} having {{e|The Stain}} in Chapter 2 and the {{e|Tuff Twins}} having {{e|The Shell}} in [[Chapters|Chapter 2.5]].
*** However, Chub and the Carrion Queen don't share the same environment, as Chub can appear in The {{s|Caves}} and The {{s|Flooded Caves}}, while the Carrion Queen can only appear in The {{s|Catacombs}}, where Chub cannot.
* {{dlc+|r}} She is the boss version of the {{e|Carrion Princess|text=Carrion Princesses}}.{{dlc-}}
* [[wikipedia:Carrion|Carrion]] is another word for the rotting flesh of a dead animal, often eaten by scavengers like vultures, coyotes, maggots, etc.
* The name "Carrion Queen" could be inspired by a level of [[wikipedia:Super Meat Boy|Super Meat Boy]] under the name "King Carrion".
* Despite the Carrion Queen being an invertebrate, or an animal lacking a skeleton of bones, she has a skeletal appearance.
* {{Dlc|nr}} During the death animation, Carrion Queen's head doesn't burst into blood. Instead, it flattens and instantly disappears.
* In the [[Bestiary]], she is referred to as "The Carrion Queen".

